---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Your data lives where you can write — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="LightPhotos keeps ratings, edits and thumbnails in a folder next to your photos, not in a database. This post explains why, and how the permission rules of a browser made the decision for me."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-08-10">August 10, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Your data lives where you are allowed to write</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          LightPhotos keeps ratings, edits and thumbnails in a folder next to
          your photos, not in a database. This post explains why, and how the
          permission rules of a browser made the decision for me.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 7 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          My first version kept all your ratings in one central catalog. When
          you moved a folder, the catalog could no longer find its ratings. So
          I started to save the ratings in a hidden folder next to your photos.
          Later, the browser version of the app could write only inside the
          folder you picked, so the thumbnail cache moved there too. Now your
          work goes wherever your photos go.
        </p>
        <p>
          There are two costs. A search across every folder is slow. And when
          you rename a photo, the app can no longer find its ratings and edits.
          That stays true until I build a fix.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Where you keep data decides what is easy.</strong> When the
          data is next to the photos, you can move, copy and share the photos
          with no extra work. But a search across folders is slow. A central
          database gives the opposite result.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The most limited platform taught me the most.</strong> I did
          not choose to put the cache next to the photos. The permission rules
          of the browser did not let me put it anywhere else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Design for the strictest platform early.</strong> The cache
          in the system folder worked correctly, and nobody complained. So I
          would never have found the better design on my own.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write down the disadvantage where someone will find
          it.</strong> Records that use only the filename as a key make late
          background results risky. So that warning belongs on the code that
          handles those results.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        LightPhotos has no database. When you give a photo four stars, the app
        writes a small JSON file called <code>IMG_4471.jpg.xmp</code>. It
        writes this file into a hidden <code>.lightphotos</code> folder, right
        next to <code>IMG_4471.jpg</code>. That is the whole system for saving
        your work.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first version did not work this way. If you asked me at the start,
        I would not have picked this design. I got here because two problems
        pushed me into it.
      </p>

      <h2>My first attempt</h2>
      <p>
        I started with one catalog for the whole app. The catalog recorded each
        photo by its full path on disk. It lived in the usual folder where Mac
        apps keep their data. This is the standard approach, and it works well
        for a while.
      </p>
      <p>
        But it fails in exactly the way people use a photo tool, because people
        move folders. You import a card into <code>~/Desktop/import</code> and
        go through the photos to pick the ones to keep. Then you drag the
        folder to an external drive. When you do that, every path in the
        catalog points to nothing.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 246" role="img" aria-label="Comparison: a central catalog that uses the full path as a key fails when a folder moves. Sidecar files in the folder move with the photos.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">V1 &middot; CENTRAL CATALOG</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="22" width="300" height="208" rx="10"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="50" y="44" width="200" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="150" y="63" text-anchor="middle">catalog.db in ~/Library</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="150" y1="74" x2="150" y2="112"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" d="M142 85 L158 101 M158 85 L142 101"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="168" y="97">path points to nothing</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="50" y="118" width="200" height="54" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-t" x="64" y="139">Iceland/</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="80" y="159">IMG_4471.jpg</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="150" y="194" text-anchor="middle">folder moved to an external drive</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="150" y="212" text-anchor="middle">an hour of culling is lost</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="340" y="12">V2 &middot; NEXT TO THE PHOTOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="340" y="22" width="300" height="208" rx="10"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="370" y="44" width="240" height="118" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-t" x="384" y="66">Iceland/</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="400" y="88">IMG_4471.jpg</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="400" y="110">.lightphotos/</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="408" y="118" width="190" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-t" x="418" y="136">IMG_4471.jpg.xmp</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="490" y1="162" x2="490" y2="176"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M484 176 L496 176 L490 186 Z"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="490" y="204" text-anchor="middle">move it, copy it, back it up</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="490" y="222" text-anchor="middle">the ratings move with it</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          I fixed it by saving the ratings in the same folder as the photos. Now
          the ratings and the photos always stay together.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Putting it next to the photos</h2>
      <p>
        Now when you move the folder, your ratings and edits move with it. The
        same is true when you copy it to a friend or back it up. I did not
        write any code for that. It works because your work and your photos are
        in the same place.
      </p>
      <p>A few details make it pleasant to use.</p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>The app makes the folder only when you save something.</strong>
          If you only look through someone else's photos, the app leaves
          nothing behind.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The app writes each file to a temporary copy, then renames
          it.</strong> On all common disk formats, a rename happens in one step.
          So if the app crashes partway through, you still have the previous
          version, not a half-written file.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The app never changes your original photo.</strong> This app
          is for deciding which photos to keep. So it must not change the
          photos you might keep.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Then the browser made the decision for me</h2>
      <p>
        Thumbnails take time to make, so the app saves them to disk. When
        LightPhotos ran only on the Mac, that cache lived in
        <code>~/Library/Caches</code>, where Apple tells you to put it. Then I
        made the app run in a browser tab.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 160" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: a browser tab can read and write only inside the folder that the user picked. The tab cannot reach the system cache folder or the rest of the disk.">
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="62" width="150" height="50" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="75" y="92" text-anchor="middle">Browser tab</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M150 87 C 220 87, 230 37, 296 37 M150 87 H296 M150 87 C 220 87, 230 137, 296 137"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" d="M215 46 L231 62 M231 46 L215 62 M215 124 L231 140 M231 124 L215 140"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M296 31 L296 43 L308 37 Z M296 81 L296 93 L308 87 Z M296 131 L296 143 L308 137 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="312" y="20" width="328" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="476" y="41" text-anchor="middle">~/Library/Caches &mdash; does not exist here</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="312" y="70" width="328" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="476" y="91" text-anchor="middle">The one folder you picked</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="312" y="120" width="328" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="476" y="141" text-anchor="middle">Anywhere else on disk &mdash; no permission</text>

        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          In a browser, the app can read and write only inside the folder you
          picked. With the cache anywhere else, the browser version would have
          to rebuild every thumbnail each time you opened the page.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        A browser tab cannot reach the system cache folder. So I moved the
        thumbnail cache into <code>.lightphotos</code>, next to the ratings
        that were already there. I did it only because the browser left me no
        choice. But the result was better on the Mac too.
      </p>

      <h2>What I got from it</h2>
      <p>
        Now when you move a folder, its thumbnails move with its ratings. When
        you plug the drive into another computer, the grid fills instantly. The
        app does not spend a minute rebuilding every thumbnail.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Mac and browser versions also share one cache, and that needed one
        deliberate compromise. The app recognises a cached thumbnail by the
        file size of the photo and the time it was last changed. The app rounds
        that time to the millisecond, because a browser gives the time only to
        the millisecond. The Mac reports the time to the nanosecond. Without the
        rounding, the two versions would label the same untouched photo
        differently and never find each other's thumbnails.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is one cached thumbnail per photo. So the cache of a folder can
        never have more entries than there are photos in it, and I do not need
        rules to throw old entries out. When a folder opens, I only clear out
        leftovers for deleted photos. If the app cannot write to a folder, it
        makes fresh thumbnails every time and does not bother you about it.
        Without the cache, the app is a little slower there, and I decided that
        was not worth an error message.
      </p>

      <h2>What it cost me</h2>
      <p>
        The app saves everything under the filename of the photo alone. It does
        not need the folder, because the location of the file already tells
        the app which folder it is in. This makes work across folders risky. If
        you switch folders while a background job runs, a late result could go
        to a photo with the same name in the new folder.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can change folders at any time, even while work is still running.
        Here is what happens to each kind of job when you do.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Exports keep running.</strong> Each export already knows the
          full location of the photo and its edits. So it finishes no matter
          which folder you are looking at.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The app saves your current edits first.</strong> If you moved
          a slider and did not leave the photo yet, I save that change before
          the new folder opens. So you do not lose it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Thumbnails keep running, and the app throws away late
          results.</strong> If a result for the old folder finishes after you
          leave that folder, the app drops it. So it cannot land on a photo
          with the same name.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Auto Tone stops.</strong> Photos that it already finished
          keep their changes. The app skips the rest.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bulk delete stops.</strong> It finishes the photo that it is
          moving to the trash at that moment, then stops.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I plan to fix those last two. You might start Auto Tone on a big shoot,
        or clear out a batch of rejects. It is frustrating to then sit and wait
        before you can look at another folder. I want both jobs to keep running
        the way exports do. To do this, each job would carry the full location
        of the photo it works on. Then the open folder would not matter when the
        job finishes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Also, there is no quick way to ask a question across every folder you
        ever opened. "Show me everything I rated five stars this year" would
        mean going through all of them. A central database could answer that
        in a millisecond. If that kind of search was central to the purpose of
        LightPhotos, this would be the wrong design.
      </p>
      <p>
        The disadvantage that worries me most is renaming. A small file (a
        sidecar file) holds your ratings and edits, and it finds its photo only
        by filename. If you rename <code>IMG_4471.jpg</code> to
        <code>iceland-sunset.jpg</code> in Finder, the sidecar is still called
        <code>IMG_4471.jpg.xmp</code>. Then LightPhotos no longer knows that the
        two files belong together, and your rating and edits for that photo
        seem to disappear. Renaming files is a normal thing to do, so this
        design breaks easily.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have not fixed this yet, but I know how I want to fix it. Each sidecar
        would also store a fingerprint of the original photo. This fingerprint
        (a hash) is a short code that the app calculates from the contents of
        the photo. When LightPhotos opens a folder and finds sidecars that do
        not match any photo, it would calculate the fingerprints of the photos
        in that folder. If one matches, the photo was only renamed, so the app
        renames the sidecar to match. Then your ratings and edits come back.
      </p>



